From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] WM Shell: cancel recents when its starting transition rotates
 the display

On a display that ignores orientation requests (large screens by default),
an activity that requests "nosensor" or "locked", or one exempted with
OVERRIDE_RESPECT_REQUESTED_ORIENTATION, still sets the display rotation.
When Overview is opened from such an activity while the sensor reports a
different rotation, the orientation request of the recents activity is
ignored, so WindowManager gives its launch no fixed-rotation transform and
puts the display rotation into the transition that starts recents.

RecentsController.start() does not handle a display change. It starts the
recents animation with the display rotation still in the transition, the
launcher never shows Overview, and the transition does not finish until
Home is pressed; until then only the wallpaper is visible. merge() already
refuses a display change that arrives while recents is running and cancels,
so that the default handler can play the rotation.

Do the same in start(): when a display rotates in the starting transition,
cancel the recents animation and return false, so that the next handler
animates the transition together with the rotation, as start() already
does when it cannot take a transition (no listener, or neither a recents
task nor a pausing task). Nothing waits on the canceled animation; whether
the launcher then shows Overview or stays on Home depends on how far it
got before the cancel arrived.

Seen on a tablet whose panel is naturally portrait, with Aperture
(screenOrientation="nosensor") exempted from the large-screen orientation
policy: held in landscape, opening Overview from Aperture left only the
wallpaper on screen until Home was pressed, in 3 of 3 attempts.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 .../recents/RecentsTransitionHandler.java     | 22 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libs/WindowManager/Shell/src/com/android/wm/shell/recents/RecentsTransitionHandler.java b/libs/WindowManager/Shell/src/com/android/wm/shell/recents/RecentsTransitionHandler.java
index 7702979..0d8e8f7 100644
--- a/libs/WindowManager/Shell/src/com/android/wm/shell/recents/RecentsTransitionHandler.java
+++ b/libs/WindowManager/Shell/src/com/android/wm/shell/recents/RecentsTransitionHandler.java
@@ -799,6 +799,16 @@ public class RecentsTransitionHandler implements Transitions.TransitionHandler,
                         + ") or no transition (" + (mTransition == null) + ")");
                 return false;
             }
+            // The display rotates in the transition that starts recents when an activity held
+            // the display in an orientation the sensor no longer reports and the display
+            // ignores the orientation request of the recents activity: WindowManager then gives
+            // the launch no fixed-rotation transform and rotates the display right away. Recents
+            // cannot animate a rotation, so leave the whole transition to the next handler, as
+            // merge() does for a display change that arrives later.
+            if (hasDisplayRotation(info)) {
+                cancel("display rotation in the starting transition");
+                return false;
+            }
             // First see if this is a valid recents transition.
             boolean hasPausingTasks = false;
             for (int i = 0; i < info.getChanges().size(); ++i) {
@@ -2110,6 +2120,18 @@ public class RecentsTransitionHandler implements Transitions.TransitionHandler,
         }
     };
 
+    /** Returns whether a display rotates in {@code info}. */
+    private static boolean hasDisplayRotation(TransitionInfo info) {
+        for (int i = info.getChanges().size() - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
+            final TransitionInfo.Change change = info.getChanges().get(i);
+            if (change.hasFlags(TransitionInfo.FLAG_IS_DISPLAY)
+                    && change.getStartRotation() != change.getEndRotation()) {
+                return true;
+            }
+        }
+        return false;
+    }
+
     /** Utility class to track the state of a task as-seen by recents. */
     private static class TaskState {
         WindowContainerToken mToken;
